/** @page jnireleasenotesguide jni Module Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @section jni_2_7_0 jni Module Version 2.7.0
    - Use the static JNI API for the headless graphics-environment query. Importing AWT-dependent
      spreadsheet APIs now passes checked JNI validation during source and AOT compilation.
    - Release every outstanding native wrapper during JVM shutdown, including wrappers Java has not
      garbage-collected. Explicit release, phantom cleanup, and shutdown atomically claim each pointer
      once; object construction cannot publish into an already closed cleanup handle.
      Cleanup without a caller discards destructor exceptions; explicit handler destruction continues
      to report them to the caller without throwing C++ exceptions through JNI or a destructor.
    - Keep callback program metadata valid until dispatcher cleanup, including callbacks created from
      closures whose owning program has already closed. This avoids reviving a cleared program's
      strong reference count and dereferencing it after the final closure frees it.
    - Release owned Qore callback results after converting them to Java, including temporary strings.
    - Fixed duplicate JNI reference deletion when creating Java arrays and unchecked JNI calls
      when converting Qore exceptions. Exception conversion now releases UTF string buffers and
      preserves the original Java exception if converting its fields or argument fails.
    - The main JNI test suite now returns its status through normal program teardown, allowing
      Valgrind to verify native cleanup. The separate abrupt-exit subprocess test is retained.
    - Fixed Java wrapper and provider documentation links, parameter descriptions, and JMS tables.
      Documentation builds use public QPP declarations and installed dependency indexes.
      Configure with <tt>-DQORE_JNI_STRICT_DOCS=ON</tt> to reject Doxygen warnings; RPM builds enable this check.
    - the \c write-data actions of the Excel, ODS, ODT, ODP, Word, PowerPoint, Avro, VCF, ICS and Email (\c eml-write-data)
      data providers and the JasperReports \c generate-report-data action now return the generated document as
      their output (\c data and \c record_count, plus \c format for reports); previously the generated data was
      lost when the action was run.  File-writing actions now write their file when the action completes, so
      write errors are reported by the action (requires %Qore 3.0 / DataProvider 3.9)
    - fixed Excel and ODS spreadsheet reading and writing: reading without header options returned no data, cell
      ranges past column \c Z were rejected, streaming Excel writes without headers failed, and ODS writes
      turned the first record's dates into strings and empty values into empty strings; Excel and ODS reading
      can now skip empty rows with the new \c ignore_empty option, and ODS reading no longer expands the runs of
      repeated empty rows that spreadsheet applications write to the end of a sheet
    - fixed JDBC pooled reads retaining transactions and blocking later writes or schema changes;
      explicit transactions and prepared writes retain commit/rollback semantics
    - fixed JDBC cursors retaining local JNI references across Java native-call frames, causing invalid
      reference errors or JVM crashes; statement cleanup now preserves errors and closes every handle
    - corrected Flyway action response descriptions and their integration tests
    - fixed JDBC batch update counts, empty batches, and prepared statement reuse after failed binds;
      unknown JDBC batch counts return \c -1 and count overflow raises \c JDBC-BATCH-COUNT-ERROR
    - added Kafka record headers to the \c KafkaDataProvider module: the producer sends a request's \c headers and
      the consumer delivers each record's headers, UTF-8 text as strings and other values as binaries; a producer
      request no longer needs a \c key
    - fixed a %Qore static variable mirroring a mutable Java static field holding a copy of the field's value
      taken when the class was imported; a change made on either side was invisible to the other.  The variable
      is now read from and written to the Java field, so both sides always agree
    - fixed the declared type of fields holding a Java map or list; such a value is converted to a %Qore hash or
      list only when its contents allow it (a map needs string keys) and compatibility types are not in effect,
      so the field now declares both forms and a field holding the Java object no longer conflicts with its type
    - fixed assigning a %Qore list to a Java argument, field or variable of a collection type such as
      <tt>java.util.List</tt> failing with <tt>cannot instantiate array from ...</tt>; a list is now converted to
      the target collection type, as a hash is converted to a map
    - fixed process crashes when loading or invoking generated API classes after their generating %Qore Program
      was destroyed or from a saved JAR in another process; generated classes now use validated binding handles
      for constants, functions, methods, constructors, and native class identity, and cleanup drains active calls
      before invalidating their metadata; regenerate API classes produced by older JNI versions
    - preserve object-retention callback failures during generated Java construction and publish the wrapper's
      weak object reference only after all construction steps succeed
    - fixed Java provider qualification aborting CMake configuration with
      <tt>Java provider AccessDataProvider has no qmod build target</tt> when
      <tt>QORE_BUILD_AOT_MODULES=OFF</tt>; source builds now validate after generating provider JARs, while
      AOT builds retain the required qmod dependencies
    - fixed the bundled @ref opcuadataproviderintro "OpcUaDataProvider" module's
      offline OPC UA NodeSet2 conversion: an imported node's browse path is now built from the model's
      containment hierarchy instead of from its browse name alone, so a real published model no longer
      fails to import with a duplicate endpoint id and no longer converts to a flat address space; method
      argument properties are folded into method endpoints, the export writes the hierarchy back out, and
      the generic server runtime materializes each browse path segment only once (see the
      @ref opcuadataproviderintro "OpcUaDataProvider v1.10.1"
      release notes)
    - fixed \c NoClassDefFoundError for a module class referenced by another module's method
      signatures (for example <tt>java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: qore/Qore/astparser/AstParser</tt>
      when generating Java classes for a module that uses the astparser module); a class's canonical
      \c qoremod.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i> binary name was derived by looking the owning module's namespace up in
      the consuming %Program, which cannot see a module's private (non-reexported) dependencies -
      those classes were then published under the legacy \c qore.<i>class-path</i> name, which no
      classloader resolves; the name is now derived from the class's own namespace, so it is the same
      in every context
    - added declarative Java provider dependency profiles generated from each module's runtime classpath directives;
      source, AOT, and installed trees now validate committed checksums, SLF4J API/provider compatibility, service
      entries, duplicate providers, logging bridge cycles, and exact staged JAR content before every provider is
      loaded in an isolated fresh process
    - fixed JVM-primary processes aborting or hanging at exit, after the Java code had already
      completed successfully, with the message <tt>external native threads still active while
      stopping the cleanup worker</tt>; the system classloader's JVM shutdown hook was never
      registered when the JVM owns the process, and now runs the documented \c qore_cleanup()
      teardown, so %Qore \c ThreadPool workers and the process-wide async I/O controller are
      stopped before libqore's static destructors run
    - fixed empty %Qore lists converting to Java \c null when passed as objects or nested in hashes and lists;
      they now become empty \c Object[] arrays, allowing empty OPC UA address spaces to be described and
      exported as NodeSet2 XML (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5446">issue 5446</a>)
    - allow empty trailing argument slices when converting typed Java varargs in Debug builds
    - check Java exception metadata calls before validating their local references, avoiding JNI checker
      warnings during conversion failures and preserving the original exception if \c getMessage() throws
    - release temporary Java class wrappers when recursive or duplicate class resolution reuses an existing
      class, including exception-safe cleanup before the new class takes ownership
    - fixed Java exceptions with native stack frames aborting Debug Qore: unavailable Java line numbers
      are normalized to the Qore unknown-location sentinel while retaining exception and frame metadata
    - <b>Added explicit shared identity for dynamically projected %Qore classes</b>
      - <b>Symptom.</b> Passing an object between two Java-enabled %Programs could fail with
        \c ClassCastException even when the object's projected class inherited the requested type; the exception
        named two sibling \c QoreURLClassLoader instances for the same Java binary name.
      - <b>Cause.</b> Parent delegation already preserves identity along a classloader ancestry chain, but separate
        %Program loaders are siblings.  When an application intentionally exposes the same non-module %Qore API
        class in multiple Programs, each sibling could dynamically define its own Java \c Class object.
      - <b>Fix.</b> The new \c register-shared-class module command lets an application opt an exact
        \c qore.* or \c qoremod.* name into one process-wide identity before it is loaded.  The first registering
        Program owns the definition; sibling loaders delegate to it.  Unregistered same-path classes remain
        Program-local, preserving applications whose implementations intentionally differ.
    - <b>Fixed the process aborting during <tt>exit()</tt> when the jni module is loaded</b>
      - <b>Symptom.</b> A program that loaded the jni module and then called \c exit() aborted
        instead of exiting, losing the exit status the script had set; on macOS the process died
        with <tt>libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type std::system_error: mutex
        lock failed: Invalid argument</tt>.  On Linux the same defect was silent, so it appeared to
        affect only macOS.
      - <b>Cause.</b> The module namespace cache and the lock protecting it were objects with static
        storage duration in the module.  Objects with static storage duration are destroyed in
        reverse order of construction, and the module's program-cleanup callback - which purges that
        cache - runs from the destructor of an object constructed earlier, so the cache and its lock
        were destroyed first and then used by that later destructor.  glibc silently tolerates
        locking a destroyed mutex; macOS returns \c EINVAL, which libc++ turns into an uncaught
        exception.
      - <b>Fix.</b> The cache and its lock are now allocated once and never destroyed, so they stay
        usable for the entire life of the process.
    - <b>Fixed Java objects passed to a %Qore callback not matching their declared %Qore class</b>
      - <b>Symptom.</b> Assigning an argument of a %Qore callback implementing a Java interface to a
        variable declared with the argument's own Java class failed with
        <tt>RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR: &lt;lvalue&gt; expects type 'object&lt;<i>X</i>&gt;', but got an object
        of class '<i>X</i>'</tt> &mdash; naming the same class on both sides.  Affected classes loaded
        from a dynamically added JAR; where the exception was caught and logged, the only visible
        effect was that callbacks silently stopped delivering data.
      - <b>Cause.</b> \c QoreCodeDispatcher bound to the thread-current %Program at construction
        instead of the %Program owning the callback.  The %Program established for the call decides
        which %Program's JNI class map wraps the arguments, so when the two differed, the class map
        built a second %Qore class for the same Java class; because that %Program's classloader
        cannot see dynamically added JARs, types in its signature that referred to classes from those
        JARs were relaxed to \c auto.  The resulting class does not compare equal to the one the
        callback's declared types refer to.
      - <b>Fix.</b> The dispatcher now binds to the %Program that owns the callback, taken at
        construction while the callback is known to be alive, so the callback always executes in the
        %Program its code was compiled in and Java arguments resolve to the classes that %Program
        imported.
    - <b>Java classes that cannot be loaded while building a class are now reported</b>
      - <b>Symptom.</b> A type appearing in a Java signature could be silently relaxed to \c auto,
        producing a %Qore class that looks identical to one built elsewhere but does not compare equal
        to it, so objects of one did not satisfy declared types of the other.  Nothing was reported in
        release builds.
      - <b>Fix.</b> The relaxation is unchanged &mdash; it must not be fatal, since Java itself loads
        classes whose unused signatures mention absent types, and optional dependencies are common
        &mdash; but it is now reported to \c stderr once per Java class, naming the class, the
        underlying Java exception and the consequence.
    - <b>Fixed dynamic Java classes binding to another Program's class of the same name</b>
      - <b>Symptom.</b> Java code calling a \c qore.<i>X</i>.<i>Y</i>... API could be bound to a
        same-named class belonging to an unrelated %Program in the process, so calls dispatched to
        the wrong implementation.  In Qorus this bound server-side Java interfaces to the
        \c QorusClient variant of \c OMQ::UserApi::UserApi, which failed at runtime with
        <tt>no pseudo method &lt;nothing&gt;::%getClientLogger()</tt>.
      - <b>Cause.</b> A \c qore.<i>X</i>.<i>Y</i>... binary name addresses a namespace path, and two
        %Programs in one process can legitimately hold different classes at the same path.
        Canonical classloader resolution scanned every loaded user-module %Program for that path
        before consulting the calling %Program, so it bound to whichever module happened to be
        iterated first &mdash; and cached that answer process-wide, keyed by the binary name alone,
        for every later caller.
      - <b>Fix.</b> The class is now resolved from the calling %Program's own view first and routed
        to that class's source %Program, which keeps a single Java \c Class per %Qore class across
        all %Programs that import it.  The module scan remains as a fallback for callers that
        cannot see the class at all, and caller-dependent results are no longer written to the
        process-wide cache.  Built-in and module-provided classes are unaffected.
    - <b>Fixed a deadlock between Java class import and concurrent module loading</b>
      - <b>Symptom.</b> A process could hang with many threads blocked while loading modules, most
        visibly at startup when several services were initialized at once.  Any Java class import
        racing a cold module load could trigger it; once hit, all further module loading in the
        process stopped, so HTTP listeners stopped responding and shutdown could not complete.
      - <b>Cause.</b> Importing a Java class takes the module's global class-map lock, and doing so
        can load a module &mdash; directly, or indirectly when the JVM classloader calls back into
        %Qore to generate byte code.  Meanwhile a cold module load holds libqore's module-load lock
        while applying the module's AOT module commands, which import Java classes and so need the
        class-map lock.  The two locks could be taken in either order, so two threads could each
        end up holding the lock the other needed.
      - <b>Fix.</b> The module now acquires libqore's module-load lock before the class-map lock on
        every path that can reach module loading, giving both orders a single, consistent
        hierarchy.  Cache-hit paths that cannot load a module or call into Java keep taking the
        class-map lock alone, so ordinary Java class resolution is not serialized; debug builds
        assert that this remains true.
    - <b>Breaking change: module classes are exposed only under \c qoremod.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i></b>
      - <b>What changed.</b> %Qore module classes are now exposed in Java under exactly one
        canonical binary name: \c qoremod.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i>.  Older module-jni versions
        also accepted the legacy \c qore.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i> shape (e.g.
        \c qore.SqlUtil.AbstractTable resolving to the \c SqlUtil module's \c AbstractTable),
        but doing so produced two distinct Java \c Class objects backed by the same
        \c QoreClass.  At runtime that aliasing surfaced as
        \c LinkageError("loader constraint violation") on subclass / parent override
        resolution and \c ClassCastException on legitimate casts across the boundary.
      - <b>What now happens.</b> Looking up \c qore.<i>X</i>.<i>Y</i> for a module-owned
        class raises \c ClassNotFoundException; the message points at the canonical
        \c qoremod.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i> form to use instead.  Built-in
        \c qore.Qore.<i>...</i> classes (e.g. \c qore.Qore.SQL.AbstractDatasource) and
        per-Program runtime-namespace classes (e.g. \c qore.OMQ.<i>...</i> in Qorus,
        codegen-test fixture classes) keep working unchanged.
      - <b>What you must do.</b> If you have pre-compiled Java bytecode or Java source that
        imports \c qore.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i> for a module-owned class, re-emit / re-import
        it as \c qoremod.<i>mod</i>.<i>rest</i>.  In practice this is one mechanical
        substitution per affected import statement; the qjar / qjava2jar / docs build
        toolchain and any code generated by current module-jni already produce the canonical
        form, so a clean rebuild is sufficient.
      - <b>Migration helper.</b> A source-level rewrite tool is shipped with the module:
        @code{.py}
qjava-migrate-imports --dry-run -v <java-source-dir>          # preview rewrites
qjava-migrate-imports --in-place --backup <java-source-dir>   # apply, .bak per file
        @endcode
        It rewrites \c qore.<i>X</i>.<i>Y</i> to \c qoremod.<i>X</i>.<i>Y</i> for every
        leading segment <i>X</i> that isn't on a keep-list (default: \c Qore, \c OMQ).
        Use \c --keep=<i>A</i>,<i>B</i>,... to extend the keep-list when your codebase
        uses additional per-Program runtime namespaces under \c qore.<i>...</i>.  Run with
        \c --dry-run first so you can audit the rewrites against your loaded-modules list
        (\c qore -l <i>mod</i> \c -ne \c "" verifies a module is loadable).
      - <b>Why a hard cut, not a deprecation flag.</b> Keeping the legacy form on a
        compatibility flag would re-introduce the dual-Class problem: every loader chain
        that needs the same module type would still independently produce two parallel
        \c Class objects, leaving the loader-constraint and cast errors in place.  The
        canonical-only design is what ensures cross-loader instances of the same QoreClass
        share one Java identity.
      - <b>Qore generic class signatures.</b> When built against Qore headers exposing
        \c QORE_HAVE_GENERIC_CLASSES, generated Java classes now preserve parameterized
        Qore parent, interface, method return, and method argument types in their generic
        signatures.  For example, Java code can compile against
        <tt>qore.Qore.AbstractIterator&lt;String&gt;</tt> when the underlying Qore class is
        <tt>AbstractIterator&lt;string&gt;</tt>.  The build keeps compatibility with plain
        Qore releases that do not expose this API by falling back to raw Java class
        signatures.
      - <b>Cross-loader sharing.</b> Even with the canonical name, a class can still get
        loaded into multiple \c QoreURLClassLoader instances (qjar, docs builds, and Qorus
        workflow Programs each create their own).  module-jni now defines each
        \c qoremod.<i>mod</i>.<i>...</i> class in exactly one place — the
        \c QoreURLClassLoader of the owning user module's QoreProgram — and every consumer
        Program delegates \c loadClass / \c define-class for that prefix to it.  The
        module's Program lazily acquires a JNI classloader on first access, so modules that
        don't \c \%requires \c jni themselves still have a single canonical home for their
        Java classes.  \c qore.Qore.<i>...</i> built-ins (which have no owning user Program)
        keep routing through the process-wide system classloader (\c Globals::syscl).
        Genuinely per-Program names (\c qore.<i>X</i>.<i>Y</i> with X ≠ \c Qore, single-
        segment \c qore.<i>X</i>) stay per-loader by design.
    - added Java wrapper methods for columnar DBI results:
      \c org.qore.lang.AbstractDatasource.selectColumnar(),
      \c org.qore.lang.AbstractDatasource.vselectColumnar(), and
      \c org.qore.lang.AbstractSQLStatement.fetchColumnar()
    - added native \c jdbc DBI support for \c Datasource::selectColumnar() and \c SQLStatement::fetchColumnar()
      when built against %Qore versions exposing the columnar result API; fixed-width JDBC result columns are returned
      as dense nullable %Qore buffers; \c NUMERIC / \c DECIMAL values with precision <= 38 are returned as packed
      \c buffer<*decimal128> columns when built against %Qore versions exposing the decimal128 buffer API, while
      date/time values retain logical schema metadata and use microsecond dense temporal storage when linked with
      %Qore dense temporal support; binary and driver-specific values retain schema metadata and fall back to list
      storage where needed
    - optimized native \c jdbc columnar reads for packed result sets with a Java-side batch reader; supported
      integer, floating-point, boolean, string, and decimal columns are extracted into primitive Java arrays in
      chunks and imported into %Qore buffers, avoiding per-cell JNI/JDBC getter calls while preserving null bitmaps,
      \c CHAR trimming, and the mixed-column fallback path for driver-specific object values
    - improved the @ref jdbcdataproviderintro "JdbcDataProvider"
      module so provider and connection options can load JDBC driver JARs with \c classpath
      and explicitly register drivers with \c driver_class before opening a connection
    - added the @ref jakartajmsdataproviderintro "JakartaJmsDataProvider"
      module for Jakarta JMS 3.x messaging brokers
      - supports ActiveMQ Artemis, TIBCO EMS, IBM MQ, Oracle AQ, and any Jakarta Messaging 3.x
        compliant provider
      - automatic Qore-to-JMS type mapping (\c string &harr; \c TextMessage, \c hash &harr;
        \c MapMessage, \c binary &harr; \c BytesMessage)
      - send (fire-and-forget), request-reply, queue browsing, and event-driven consumption
      - JMS SQL-92 message selector support
      - durable topic subscriptions with \c client_id
      - thread-safe JMS session pool
      - connection provider with \c jms scheme
      - 4 app actions: \c send-jms-message, \c jms-request-reply, \c browse-jms-queue,
        \c jms-message-received
    - added the @ref opcuadataproviderintro "OpcUaDataProvider" module for OPC UA
      connectivity using the Eclipse Milo 0.6.13 SDK
      - supports data change subscriptions via the observer pattern
      - supports read/write node values, browse address space, and call methods via the request API
      - supports connection provider with \c opcua, \c opcuatcp, and \c opcuahttps schemes
      - supports SSL/TLS and OPC UA security policy configuration
      - includes action catalog integration (7 actions)
    - fixed a bug in \c QoreCodeDispatcher::dispatch() where \c callback->getProgram() could return
      a null pointer, causing a SIGSEGV when Java callback threads invoked Qore code via
      \c implement_interface(); the dispatcher now checks for null before accessing the program's
      external data
    - fixed a bug where Java callback threads using module method references would fail to resolve
      classes because the callback's program context lacked JNI external data; the dispatcher now
      falls back to the program captured at \c QoreInvocationHandler construction time
    - fixed \c addParentClasspath() to fall back to adding JARs to the classloader directly when the
      parent classloader is not a \c QoreURLClassLoader (bootstrap program case)
    - fixed \c getQoreType() to gracefully handle class loading failures during type resolution by
      falling back to \c auto type instead of aborting class population
    - fixed \c MqttDataProvider to bundle the \c org.eclipse.paho.mqttv5.common jar alongside the
      \c org.eclipse.paho.mqttv5.client jar; the Paho MQTTv5 client jar contains only the
      \c org.eclipse.paho.mqttv5.client package and depends on the separate \c common artifact at
      runtime, so loading the module previously failed with
      \c ClassNotFoundException: org.eclipse.paho.mqttv5.common.MqttSubscription
    - fixed dynamic Java class generation for mutually-referential %Qore class clusters (for example
      \c DataProvider's \c AbstractDataProviderType / \c QoreDataType / \c HashDataType), which could
      fail at generation or static-initialization time with
      \c ClassNotFoundException: "<class> is already being created" when a base class declares a method
      whose parameter or return type is a not-yet-generated subclass:
      - the owning %Qore class is now marked creation-in-progress before any method-signature or
        constant materialization, so self/cyclic type references resolve to forward type references;
      - a referenced class whose superclass chain contains an in-progress class likewise resolves to a
        forward reference instead of re-entering generation;
      - the superclass handed to Byte Buddy is described from bytecode via a \c TypePool (new
        \c org.qore.jni.QoreClassFileLocator) so that computing the method graph and the
        super-constructor invocation does not eagerly reflect on loaded classes and recursively
        re-enter generation of an in-progress class
    - upgraded the bundled Byte Buddy library from 1.14.19 to 1.18.10, which natively supports newer
      Java class file versions; this is required for the bytecode-backed superclass description above
    - simplified \c Jni::org::qore::jni::check_network_bind() to resolve the active sandbox across the
      thread's program-context (call) stack, so module/library code invoked from a sandboxed program
      correctly inherits the caller's sandbox without needing an owner object (requires a %Qore release
      that resolves the sandbox manager across the program-context stack)
    - fixed an ABBA deadlock between the global class-map lock and the per-Program code-generation lock
      that could hang concurrent %Qore-to-Java byte code generation and Java-to-%Qore class import (for
      example during parallel service startup); the global class-map lock is now acquired strictly before
      the per-Program code-generation lock on all class-generation paths
    - fixed a related Java classloader monitor deadlock during concurrent dynamic bytecode generation and
      Java class import by avoiding native bytecode generation while holding Java class-loading locks
    - fixed intermittent native crashes during recursive dynamic Java class generation by keeping Java string
      references valid for the complete lifetime of native UTF string buffers
    - fixed recursive dynamic Java class generation when a consumer %Program inherits a
      module-owned class; bytecode lookup now routes shared classes through the owning module's
      canonical classloader, matching normal class loading
    - fixed process-exit aborts after dynamic Java bytecode generation by preventing deferred
      destruction of the JNI global %Program after %Qore synchronization state has been finalized

    @section jni_2_6_0 jni Module Version 2.6.0
    - added Kotlin language integration support
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5131">issue 5131</a>)
      - added \c kotlin.* and \c kotlinmod.* package prefixes for dynamic Kotlin class imports
      - added automatic filtering of Kotlin synthetic members (\c ACC_SYNTHETIC, \c ACC_BRIDGE)
      - added automatic filtering of Kotlin default parameter methods (\c $default variants)
      - added automatic filtering of Kotlin companion object classes (\c $Companion)
      - added \c KotlinMetadataHelper Java class for detecting Kotlin classes via <tt>&#64;kotlin.Metadata</tt> annotation
      - added \c qkotlinc helper script for compiling Kotlin sources that use Qore APIs
      - added \c kotlin_eval() function for runtime Kotlin scripting (requires kotlin-scripting-jsr223 JARs)
      - added \c kotlin_scripting_available() function to check if scripting is available
      - added \c kotlin_scripting_retry() function to retry scripting initialization after loading JARs
      - added \c kotlin-eval module command for parse-time Kotlin evaluation
      - added \c download-kotlin-scripting-jars script to download required JARs from Maven
      - Kotlin's \c HashMap, \c LinkedHashMap, and other \c Map implementations can be passed directly
        to %Qore methods expecting \c hash<auto> parameters; note that Java code using generated
        bytecode for \c hash<auto> parameters will now see \c java.util.Map instead of
        \c org.qore.jni.Hash in method signatures
      - Kotlin/Java lists are converted to the most specific %Qore list type based on element types
        (e.g., \c List<Integer> converts to \c list<int>); note that this means appending values of
        different types to such lists will throw a runtime type error
      - Kotlin/Java maps are converted to the most specific %Qore hash value type based on value types
        (e.g., \c Map<String,Integer> converts to \c hash<string,int>); note that this means adding
        values of different types to such hashes will throw a runtime type error
      - automatic conversion of \c java.time.LocalDateTime and \c java.time.Instant to %Qore \c date type;
        note that \c LocalDateTime has no timezone and will be interpreted as a local/floating date-time,
        while \c Instant is always UTC
    - added Kotlin integration tests
    - added default JVM native access enablement for unnamed modules; use
      \c QORE_JNI_DISABLE_NATIVE_ACCESS=1 to suppress the option
    - added \c native_access_option_enabled() to report native access enablement status

    @section jni_2_5_0 jni Module Version 2.5.0
    - added support for interruptible I/O for graceful termination of sandboxed programs; interrupt
      checks are performed before JVM thread attachment
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5116">issue 5116</a>)
    - <b>breaking change</b>: updated to require Java 21+ for compatibility with Java 18+ where \c finalize() is
      deprecated for removal
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5034">issue 5034</a>)
    - replaced deprecated \c finalize() methods with \c java.lang.ref.Cleaner API in \c QoreObjectBase,
      \c QoreClosure, \c QoreExceptionWrapper, and \c QoreInvocationHandler; this change makes calling
      \c release() or \c destroy() multiple times safe (idempotent)
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5034">issue 5034</a>)
    - fixed a CMake bug where inner class inc file dependencies were not tracked correctly due to a typo
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5034">issue 5034</a>)
    - fixed a bug where Java compiler diagnostics were output to stderr instead of being captured in the
      exception message
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4925">issue 4925</a>)
    - improved \c QoreURLClassLoader thread safety by using \c ConcurrentHashMap for all caches
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5047">issue 5047</a>)
    - added \c getValidPtr() method to \c QoreURLClassLoader that throws an exception if the program
      pointer is invalid
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5045">issue 5045</a>)
    - added \c clearContext() method to \c QoreURLClassLoader for explicit thread context cleanup
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5040">issue 5040</a>)
    - added \c clearAllCaches() method to \c QoreURLClassLoader for comprehensive cache cleanup
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5042">issue 5042</a>)
    - \c QoreJavaCompiler now implements \c AutoCloseable for proper resource cleanup;
      usage with try-with-resources:
      @code{.java}
      try (QoreJavaCompiler compiler = new QoreJavaCompiler(classLoader, options)) {
          compiler.compile(className, sourceCode);
      } // compiler.close() is called automatically
      @endcode
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5038">issue 5038</a>)
    - added validation of program pointer before use in native calls to prevent crashes with invalid pointers
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5039">issue 5039</a>)
    - improved \c clearCompilationCache() to also clear the \c classes cache
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5042">issue 5042</a>)
    - fixed abstract method resolution for Java classes imported at runtime through Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5056">issue 5056</a>)

    @section jni_2_4_0 jni Module Version 2.4.0
    - added the @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider" module to allow for reading
      record-based data from Excel spreadsheets
    - added the @ref kafkadataproviderintro "KafkaDataProvider" module to allow for to support
      Kafka messaging
    - @ref mqttdataproviderintro "MqttDataProvider" module
      - added support for the data provider action catalog API
        (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4808">issue 4808</a>)
    - fixed handling conversions from %Qore and %Python to Java for \c Float values
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4890">issue 4890</a>)
    - fix for importing Qore methods with varargs into Java
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4833">issue 4833</a>)

    @section jni_2_3_1 jni Module Version 2.3.1
    - fixed a bugs handling the signal mask that could cause hard-to-debug problems such as deadlocks in JIT in the
      JVM on macOS aarch64
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4793">issue 4793</a>)
    - fixed signatures for Java methods with varargs params
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4787">issue 4787</a>)
    - fixed a bug where %Qore's thread stack size settings were not applied to Java threads
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4769">issue 4769</a>)

    @section jni_2_3_0 jni Module Version 2.3.0
    - added the @ref mqttdataproviderintro "MqttDataProvider" module to allow for publishing
      and subscribing to MQTT messages
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4756">issue 4756</a>)

    @section jni_2_2_1 jni Module Version 2.2.1
    - fixed a bug handling class loaders in JDBC connection acquisition when called from %Qore code imported in native
      Java code; in this case dynamic dispatch is used in the internal call to \c DriverManager.getConnection() to
      ensure that the call succeeds
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4740">issue 4740</a>)

    @section jni_2_2_0 jni Module Version 2.2.0
    - implemented support for specifying the minimum an maximum heap sizes for the JVM on initialization from
      environment variables
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4734">issue 4734</a>)
    - fixed bugs handling dynamic Java class generation from %Qore classes with abstract methods
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4737">issue 4737</a>)

    @section jni_2_1_1 jni Module Version 2.1.1
    - fixed reading date/time values from MS SQL Server databases
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4705">issue 4705</a>)
    - fixed exception handling in destroying JNI program data
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4704">issue 4704</a>)

    @section jni_2_1_0 jni Module Version 2.1.0
    - added support for the @ref jdbc_driver "jdbc DBI driver"
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4684">issue 4684</a>)
    - fixed a bug where \c qjavac did not detect the binary path of compiled objects correctly in all cases
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4683">issue 4683</a>)
    - fixed a bug in \c qjavac where error information was not output when compiler diagnostic information was missing
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4682">issue 4682</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_11 jni Module Version 2.0.11
    - updated to work with the latest \c qpp from %Qore 1.12.4+

    @section jni_2_0_10 jni Module Version 2.0.10
    - fixed a bug where the dynamic compiler (@ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler "QoreJavaCompiler") could
      not load classes in internal Java modules
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4619">issue 4619</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_9 jni Module Version 2.0.9
    - added the following new class to the Java API:
      - @ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosureMarkerImpl
    - fixed a bug where a crash could result when calling a Java varargs method from another language with no
      arguments passed
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4594">issue 4594</a>)
    - fixed a bug importing %Qore APIs using the \c code type into Java as @ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosureMarkerImpl types
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4593">issue 4593</a>)
    - fixed a bug handling %Qore constructor calls from dynamically generated Java classes
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4581">issue 4581</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_8 jni Module Version 2.0.8
    - fixed a bug importing %Qore abstract methods with default or optional arguments
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4570">issue 4570</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_7 jni Module Version 2.0.7
    - fixed a bug where missing c++ exception handling could cause a crash
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4500">issue 4500</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_6 jni Module Version 2.0.6
    - fixed a bug where \c qjavac wrote output files in the current working directory instead of in the directory of
      the input file
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4376">issue 4376</a>)
    - fixed a bug where %Qore and %Python functions could not be imported into the special \c $Functions Java class if
      the enclosing namespace had less than two classes in it
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4375">issue 4375</a>)
    - fixed a bug where @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader could not be used as the system class loader
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4374">issue 4374</a>)
    - fixed a bug where %Qore and %Python dynamic module imports were not created with consistent Java binary names;
      also to facilitate handling injected symbols, the \c mark-module-injected module command was implemented
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4373">issue 4373</a>)
    - fixed a bug where Java method args in array format were not always converted from %Qore values correctly
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4372">issue 4372</a>)
    - fixed a bug where the %Qore \c Program and Java \c ClassLoader context was not always set correctly when
      instantiating %Qore classes from Java when there were dependencies on dynamic classes, leading to class
      resolution errors
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4371">issue 4371</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_5 jni Module Version 2.0.5
    - fixed a memory error in exception handling in %Qore to Java tyype conversions that could cause a core dump
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4349">issue 4349</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_4 jni Module Version 2.0.4
    - fixed a bug handling %Qore callbacks in native Java threads
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4340">issue 4340</a>)
    - fixed a bug handling Java interfaces as parents of a Java class
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4337">issue 4337</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_3 jni Module Version 2.0.3
    - implemented the magic \c '$' target when importing %Qore or %Python modules with import statements with the
      \c qoremod or \c pythonmod special packages
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4304">issue 4304</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_2 jni Module Version 2.0.2
    - fixed a bug where vararg arguments were not handled correctly when dynamically-generated Java called a %Qore or
      %Python static method
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4302">issue 4302</a>)
    - fixed a bug where a runtime error was generated when used with Java 17 or above
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4300">issue 4300</a>)

    @section jni_2_0_1 jni Module Version 2.0.1
    - fixed a bug where parameters of %Qore type \c null or \c nothing would cause runtime errors when importing
      the method into Java
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4287">issue 4287</a>)
    - fixed static method calls of imported %Qore methods in Java; the calling context is now set correctly
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4286">issue 4286</a>)
    - fixed importing abstract %Qore methods into Java; Java methods are no longer marked as taking trailing vararg
      arguments
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4282">issue 4282</a>)
    - fixed importing %Qore classes with multiple inheritance into Java; methods from other non-primary base classes
      are now also imported into the dynamic Java class
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4091">issue 4091</a>)

    @section jni_2_0 jni Module Version 2.0
    - %Qore @ref int_type "int" values are now converted to Java \c long in order to maintain precision
    - the old hand-developed Java wrapper classes are deprecated as of this release in favor of
      @ref jni_dynamic_import_in_java "dynamic imports"
    - implemented support for dynamically importing %Qore code into Java
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4091">issue 4091</a>)

    @section jni_1_2 jni Module Version 1.2
    - fixed crashes handling deleted Qore program contexts
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4263">issue 4262</a>)
    - fixed crashes handling Java lambdas wrapping Qore closures and call references
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4261">issue 4261</a>)
    - fixed importing AWT classes in headless mode
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4231">issue 4231</a>)
    - fixed method calls for methods that return base types where a caller context is required (for Reflection access
      control, for example)
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4093">issue 4093</a>)
    - fixed a memory leak (or an assertion crash in debug mode) for a duplicate member error when importing Java
      classes in %Qore
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4029">issue 4029</a>)
    - added support for converting %Qore closures / call references to a Java object
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/3995">issue 3995</a>)

    @section jni_1_1_3 jni Module Version 1.1.3
